<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan José Linz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Juan José Linz Storch de Gracia, Juan Linz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan José Linz was a German-born Spanish sociologist and political scientist of comparative politics, best known for his work on authoritarian regimes and democratization. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, he went with his mother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1932 and took a doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> in 1959; he taught at Columbia from 1961 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> from 1969 for the rest of his life. His study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s Spain classed it as an authoritarian regime, when regimes tended to be called either democratic or totalitarian. His works include Totalitarian and Authoritarian Regimes (1975), The Perils of Presidentialism (1990) and, with Alfred Stepan, Problems of Democratic Transition and Consolidation (1996).</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The political scientist <b>Juan José Linz</b>, defining authoritarian rule, named its features as power held by a single leader or a small group and exercised within ill-defined limits, limited political pluralism and limited mass mobilisation.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Juan Linz</b> argued in 1990 that parliamentary systems, which can replace a leader without a constitutional crisis, are less likely to break down than presidential ones, and Alfred Stepan and Cindy Skach found that new democracies with parliamentary constitutions survived far more often than presidential ones.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars who reject the claim, associated with the political scientist <b>Juan Linz</b>, that presidentialism itself endangers democracy argue that its record depends on its design and setting.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in New Haven, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.74,7.1&amp;year=1982" aria-label="Bonn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4712 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan José Linz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5162 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5162 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.74,7.1&amp;year=1982">What was here in 1982? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
